 note = {Ancient Meme Protocols is a performative installation and ‘reverse-xenoarchaeological’ field report that imagines extra-terrestrial archaeologists excavating the digital debris of 21st-century human civilization. Staged as an open dig site, two ‘researchers’ periodically ‘publish’ their findings through live rap and multimedia performance, revealing the aliens’ speculative misreading of memes, digital information, and technological objects: a mechanically automated noise generator, presented as the only remaining ‘functional’ electronic device from human civilization; monitor residues displaying curated image collections; playlists from trending streaming platforms and social media posts interpreted as evidence of human digital lifestyles; defunct QR codes decoded as cryptic messages; and a Buddhist scripture speaker, a megaphone storing audio ciphers, kiddie rides, and a rice cooker, all analysed as ‘religious artifacts’.

Filtered through the duo’s artistic research into speculative storytelling and perspectives as East Asian artists based in Europe, Ancient Meme Protocols questions whether defamiliarizing everyday rituals—transforming them into absurd yet tender relics—can foster new modes of transcultural dialogue. It also asks what kind of narratives might emerge from a conversation no one is alive to join.},
